osition & Betting:**\n- I have 19,950 chips (lost 50 to big blind)\n- Valid actions include: Fold, Check, Raise (minimum 200), or All-in\n- Since I can check, this means my opponent has not raised yet - likely they just called the big blind\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n\n1. **Hand Strength:** A-Q offsuit is strong enough to play aggressively. It dominates many hands and has good equity against most ranges.\n\n2. **Check (0):** This is passive and gives my opponent a free look at the flop. With a premium hand like A-Q, I'm missing value by not building the pot.\n\n3. **Raise (200+):** A standard raise of 200-400 chips (2-4x the big blind) would:\n   - Build the pot with a strong hand\n   - Get information about opponent's hand strength\n   - Potentially win the pot immediately if they fold\n   - Maintain a reasonable stack-to-pot ratio for post-flop play\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Too aggressive for hand 1 of 20. This risks my entire stack on a strong but not premium hand (like AA or KK). It's exploitable and unnecessarily risky this early.\n\n5. **Fold (-1):** Absurd with A-Q. This is way too strong to fold pre-flop, especially when I can check for free.\n\n**Best Move:** A standard raise to establish control and build the pot with a strong hand.\n\nReason: A♥ Q♦ is a premium starting hand that should be played aggressively pre-flop. A raise of 300 chips (3x big blind) is a solid, standard play that builds the pot while maintaining flexibility for post-flop play. This maximizes value from my strong hand while keeping risk manageable in the first hand of 20.\n\nAnswer: 300"}
